All 33 are listed here rather than cross-referenced. The "STAGED OUTPUTS" block
the older text pointed at records a different encoding of the same objects -
EI_DATA 1, e_machine 0x1051 - so not one of its 28 hashes is a base.7z hash.

Every one of these except cgi/libcgi.a has a provenance chain back to the 2001
originals in this archive, and the chain is one transform: rewrite e_machine
from 0x7262 to 165 in place, as a big-endian halfword, and upper-case every
SHT_STRTAB that a SHT_SYMTAB links to. EI_DATA stays 2. Nothing else is
touched - no other header field, no section header, no symbol entry, no
relocation.

Under that transform all 28 loose objects in this archive reproduce their
base.7z copies byte-for-byte. The archives reproduce as well, once the two
things the archive itself needs are applied - the armap's name block
upper-cased, and the ar headers put in GNU ar's deterministic form (date, uid
and gid zeroed, object members' mode 644), which is what tb/i51elf_ar.py
already does on the header side. On that footing lib/libk80.a, lib/libk23.a,
lib/libw23.a and lib/libw80.a all come out byte-identical to base.7z's.
cgi/libcgi.a is the one input that does not reproduce - section 4.

The scripts in tb/ do not implement that transform as they stand.
tb/i51elf_le2be.py converts to little-endian: run here on this archive's
cgi/bd.obj it wrote an EI_DATA = 1 object hashing 52cbea8c..., where base.7z's
cgi/bd.obj is EI_DATA = 2 and hashes 702ced5b.... tb/i51elf_sym_uc.py and
tb/i51elf_ar.py sit downstream of it. Repairing them is a separate change; the
transform stated above is what the bytes in base.7z actually record.

lib/libk23.a and lib/libw23.a in the overlay are byte-identical to
lib/lib23_old/libk23.a and lib/lib23_old/libw23.a already shipped inside
base.7z - 8a36508f... and 2189cf4f... on both sides - which is a second,
independent copy of the same 2001 files.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. KNOWN CONTENT DELTAS - overlay vs base.7z, beyond format
------------------------------------------------------------------------
One path is not a re-encoding of the other; its bytes differ beyond e_machine
and symbol case.

  cgi/libcgi.a  55792 (overlay) vs 55332 (base.7z)

Both hold 26 object members under the same 26 names, and not one of them
reproduces under the transform: every base.7z member is 4 to 32 bytes shorter
than the overlay's. base.7z's libcgi.a is a differently built archive, not a
converted copy of this one. No project links it.

lib/libk23.a and lib/libw23.a used to be listed here as not compared. They have
been compared now, and they are not deltas: both reproduce base.7z's copies
byte-for-byte under the transform in section 3.

lib/web51_80.obj, lib/web51_23.obj and lib/libw80.a used to be listed here too.
base.7z shipped re-assembled copies of them, and a re-assembly loses what the
port's assembler could not write: an external memory-space common came out as
SHN_COMMON instead of the SHN_I51_* index naming its space. Counted over the
symbol tables of this archive's copies, the three carry 0xff02 x1 + 0xff06 x6,
0xff02 x1 + 0xff06 x8, and 0xff00 x11 + 0xff02 x2 + 0xff03 x2 + 0xff06 x21
respectively. base.7z's copies now carry exactly the same census, index for
index, which is what says the re-assembled copies are gone.

Paths base.7z ships that the overlay does NOT replace, because gnu13 has no
counterpart and no project links them:

  lib/web51.obj - 10172 bytes, sha256
    96e442a85d9cae1a0a541830f7b31100920577bc06e6fbcdd732f92eb778ddb3. NOT a
    duplicate of base.7z's lib/web51_80.obj, which is 10280 bytes and hashes
    f746fff3.... It is a leftover copy of the re-assembled input the repair
    replaced: its symbol table carries no SHN_I51_* index at all, where
    web51_80.obj carries seven.
  lib/libw.a - 77790 bytes against lib/libw80.a's 78576, the same 37 member
    names, 26 of the 37 a different size, and again no SHN_I51_* index
    anywhere. The same kind of leftover.
  "cgi/$(basename  .asm).obj" - 444 bytes, left behind by a Makefile expansion.

All three are in the current container format (EI_DATA 2, e_machine 165), so
they were restamped along with the rest of base.7z; they were simply never
re-derived from the 2001 originals. Nothing opens them in a frozen run.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. WHAT THE OVERLAY DELIBERATELY LEAVES ALONE
------------------------------------------------------------------------
gnu13 also carries the .asm sources, the perl generators, include/param.inc,
lib/www51.sc and the project directories, and those differ from base.7z's.
Compared path by path: gnu13 holds 185 files, base.7z holds 286, every gnu13
path exists in base.7z, 85 of them byte-identical and 100 different.

  - .section flags were rewritten from `, #alloc` to `, "ax"` throughout the
    sources and inside bin/html2db.pl. gnu13 carries 116 `#alloc` lines across
    51 of the shared files and base.7z carries none; one of them landed as
    `, "a"` rather than `, "ax"` - reset_network in lib/packet.asm. Both
    spellings parse in 2.11.2 gas (obj_elf_section_word and
    obj_elf_parse_section_letters respectively); "ax" additionally sets
    SHF_EXECINSTR, which only affects orphan placement.
  - base.7z's sources add 14 `.local` declarations that gnu13 has no
    counterpart for, 12 of them `.local WMCON`.
  - include/param.inc uses .equ where 2001 used .set, and guards ACTIVESTACK,
    DUALSTACK and SERIAL with .ifndef - three guards, not two.
  - lib/www51.sc uppercases the interrupt trampoline symbol to _RETI_ in its
    five PROVIDE lines and adds five INTIE0.. aliases and a STACK alias beside
    the lowercase intIE0.. and stack ones. base.7z's copy is 265 lines to
    gnu13's 258: five re-spelled lines and seven added ones - the five aliases,
    the STACK alias and one trailing blank - are the whole difference.
    `*(reset_network)` is live in both, at line 82.

The overlay keeps base.7z's versions of the sources and the generators on
purpose. The reference ROMs in base.7z were linked from them, so swapping in
gnu13's would change what a frozen run is being compared against rather than
tell us anything about the toolchain. lib/www51.sc is the one exception, and
only for the symbol name - section 6.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. lib/www51.sc - THE ONE DERIVED FILE
------------------------------------------------------------------------
The overlay's copy is base.7z's own lib/www51.sc with the ten occurrences of
_RETI_ rewritten to _reti_, and nothing else. Re-run this session: base.7z's
bytes with that replacement hash to the overlay's copy exactly, and the two
files differ in 40 byte positions - ten times the four letters of RETI - and
nowhere else.

  python -c "import sys;d=open(sys.argv[1],'rb').read();
             open(sys.argv[2],'wb').write(d.replace(b'_RETI_',b'_reti_'))" \
      base.7z:lib/www51.sc  overlay:lib/www51.sc

  base.7z    3dc717e79adee15e04b03d091964a9e780f6e08615a4a5c6be9638f3d342cb1f
  overlay    1c5cfb4c8c90ef72a5875a307d8c7ee17b569a08dd91bcdaf2e901dce77655cb
  both 5623 bytes, 265 LF-terminated lines

Why. The script's ten PROVIDE lines for the unused interrupt vectors all
resolve to the trampoline in web51_80.obj / web51_23.obj. That symbol is
spelled _reti_ in the 2001 objects and _RETI_ in base.7z's - measured in both
this session: this archive's web51_80.obj (317 symbols) and web51_23.obj (327)
hold _reti_, base.7z's hold _RETI_, and the two symbol tables differ in nothing
but case. Left alone, a frozen link has no _RETI_ to resolve against and stops
on all ten projects. The exact wording of the linker's message was not re-run
here, so it is no longer quoted.

Rewriting the reference is the script-side half of the same case conversion the
objects went through; the uppercase alias names INTIE0.., which nothing in a
frozen run references, are left in place.

What was NOT done, deliberately: gnu13's own lib/www51.sc was not used, even
though it would need no _reti_ rewrite. Measured, it differs from base.7z's
copy in three things and no others: its five PROVIDE lines spell _reti_, the
five INTIE0.. aliases and the STACK alias are absent, and it has one trailing
blank line fewer - 258 LF-terminated lines against 265, 5431 bytes against
5623. `*(reset_network)` is live in both, at line 82 of each, between
*(reset_device) and *(reset_end) - no script in play omits the section.
base.7z was repacked with that line restored (tb/Makefile's oracle comment
records the repair), and tb/reference.md5 is pinned to the ten www8051.rom
files that archive ships: size and md5 agree on all ten, checked here.
Deriving the overlay's script from base.7z's by the one-line
_reti_ rewrite is therefore the smaller change: it keeps the aliases and the
line count identical, and leaves exactly one variable between the two lines -
the toolchain and the object encoding it reads.
